CLOTURE GAP + MECHANISM REQUEST, from scheduler interactive session 2026-07-27 (second drop; the first is From-scheduler-interactive-ses-20260727-102821.idea, doctrine pattern 16).

THE PATTERN: /cloture step 5 says file residue "where its owner dispatches from", and step 3 says list deferred cross-writes. Neither step VERIFIES that what was filed is actually resolvable where it was filed. "Filed" and "resolvable" are treated as the same act, and they are not. A closing session can satisfy every step of /cloture, produce a clean closeout-lint, and still have routed its residue into a place no permitted actor will ever read -- and because the lint is green and the summary says "filed", it reads as handled.

This is BUILD-DISCIPLINE pattern 13 (a decision without a dispatch path) reappearing one layer up: not in the work, but in the CLOSING RITE meant to catch it. Worth noting because /cloture is being promoted to global.

LIVE EXHIBIT, this session, committed by the session that had just proposed pattern 16 about exactly this shape: I filed two realisateur-owned items ([batch] rows: the BUILD-DISCIPLINE pattern-16 proposal, and a record gap for c49c70d) into SCHEDULER .scheduler/FOCUS.md, because realisateur was BUSY and /cloture step 3 says to list deferred cross-writes. Both were unresolvable there: bin/scheduler-dev-cycle.sh:282,284 hard-bars the scheduler cycle from touching any file outside its own repo, and realisateur nightly-batch.md explicitly says its ecosystem survey output is "not something to act on item-by-item unprompted". So the only two readers were, respectively, forbidden and instructed not to. Caught only because a human asked "clarify that the deferred work would actually be picked up" -- i.e. by a human noticing, which is the organ this whole ecosystem is trying to spend less of.

SECOND EXHIBIT, independent, found while verifying the first: realisateur own inbox currently holds 8 unconsumed .idea artifacts, oldest 2026-07-26 23:40. They accumulated because realisateur-nightly-batch lease tripped 2026-07-26 22:34 and the job was dark until renewed 2026-07-27 09:52. So anything dropped in that window was filed into a DEAD QUEUE, and nothing anywhere said so -- the drop reported "committed + pushed" and "picked up by its next nightly-batch cycle", which was false for as long as the job stayed expired. A successful-looking file operation is not a dispatch.

MECHANISM REQUESTED -- a layer-1 addition to bin/closeout-lint.sh (offline, deterministic, no claude calls, per scheduler/docs/offline-first-checks.md), so this is caught mechanically rather than by prose discipline in the command file:

  1. DEAD-QUEUE CHECK. For each project this session filed into, assert that project is actually dispatching: present in schedule/_paced.conf or _paced.dexter.conf or a crontab surface, AND its ~/.local/share/<job>/expires_at has not tripped. Filing into an expired or unregistered project is a FLAG, not a NOTE -- it is close to a verdict by construction, same class as an unpushed commit. Would have caught exhibit 2 the moment it happened. NOTE: read every dispatch surface, not one -- per pattern 14, "not in _paced.conf" is not "does not dispatch".

  2. OWNER/LOCATION MISMATCH. If a backlog row in project X names project Y as the thing to change (a path under Y, or Y own doctrine files), FLAG it: the row lives in X backlog but only Y actors may act on it. Would have caught exhibit 1.

  3. QUEUE DEPTH, reported not flagged. State how many unconsumed inbox artifacts the destination already holds and the age of the oldest. "Filed" reading as "will be handled shortly" is the actual failure of understanding; 8-deep behind a job that was dead for 11h is a different claim from 0-deep. This is the honest-uncertainty half -- it should inform the closing summary, not block it.

  4. Optional and probably the strongest: assert the destination has a READER THAT MAY ACT. Scheduler dev-cycle write scope is declarable (it is stated in its own prompt); realisateur nightly-batch declares "do not act on other projects items unprompted". If those constraints were machine-readable rather than prose in a command file, checks 2 and 4 collapse into one. Might be too big for one cycle -- 1 and 2 are the cheap high-value pair.

DOCTRINE LINE, if it survives your own review: filing is not routing. A closing rite must verify the destination has a reader who is both PERMITTED and ALIVE, or it has only moved the residue somewhere quieter.

Filed via the front door rather than a direct write, though check-project-busy now reports realisateur free -- keeping both this session drops in one inbox so they are consumed together. Note the earlier drop is the doctrine proposal itself; this one is the mechanism that would have caught its misfiling.
